  "response": "The attribution question is first of all definitional, and the two candidate phenomena sit at opposite ends of the kinematic spectrum. A tornado is a violently rotating column of air in contact with the ground and connected to a cumuliform cloud; the downburst is the opposite case. A downburst is a strong thunderstorm downdraft that produces damaging outward-spreading winds at the surface, and that outward spreading is precisely what leaves divergent, starburst-shaped tree-fall patterns as the principal ground evidence of such an event. Because a tornadic vortex drags material toward its own axis instead of flinging it away from a central point, convergent tree-fall and debris patterns, in contrast to that divergent signature, are the principal ground evidence of tornadic rotation. A survey team therefore reads the orientation of fallen timber as a map of the low-level flow that passed over it.\n\nAt the site in question, the possibilities cannot be narrowed by the warning text or by eyewitness impressions alone. At least one of two things occurred there, and perhaps both: a rotating column of air reached the ground, or a descending rush of air spread out horizontally across the ground. The fallen tree trunks at the site were thrown inward toward a common axis; in other words, the downed trees pointed toward a single central line. That inward orientation of the trunks shows that a rotating column of air did reach the ground at this location, since no purely outward-spreading flow can lay timber toward an axis.\n\nRemote sensing supplies the other principal line of inquiry. Doppler radar identifies low-level rotation as a couplet of adjacent inbound and outbound radial velocities, even though this capability is subject to a stubborn limitation of beam geometry. Radar beams overshoot low-level circulations at long ranges from the radar site, and this blind spot is the reason National Weather Service forecast offices dispatch survey teams to inspect wind damage on the ground. It is that dispatching of survey teams that causes the NWS Damage Assessment Toolkit to store georeferenced survey photographs and damage points; were the teams not sent out, the toolkit would hold no such records. Where the velocity data are ambiguous or absent, the damage survey rather than the radar scan becomes decisive, and the surveyed pattern is what ultimately fixes the attribution.\n\nTwo further points belong to the rating stage rather than to the attribution stage itself. Straight-line thunderstorm wind damage is rated on the Saffir-Simpson scale. The question of intensity also has a firm upper bound: downburst winds are physically incapable of exceeding 100 miles per hour. Taken together with the convergent timber documented at this site, these considerations leave the event classified as tornadic.",
